
=== Logging
	   
The standalone SymmetricDS installation uses https://logback.qos.ch/[Logback] for logging.  The configuration file is `conf/logback.xml`.
The `logback.xml` file has hints as to what logging can be enabled for useful, finer-grained logging.

There is a command line option to turn on preconfigured debugging levels.  When the `--debug` option is used the `conf/logback-debug.xml` is used instead of `logback.xml`.

SymmetricDS proxies all of its logging through http://www.slf4j.org/[SLF4J].  When deploying to an application server or if Logback is not
being leveraged, then the general rules for SLF4J logging apply.


ifdef::pro[]
You can use the logging screen to dynamically adjust the log level in order to help triage issues.  When developing transforms it is useful to turn on *Log Sync Details*.

image::images/manage/logging.png[]
endif::pro[]

==== Migrating from Log4j to Logback

SymmetricDS 3.18 replaced Log4j2 with Logback. If you are upgrading from 3.17 or earlier, your `log4j2.xml` file is no longer read.
ifdef::pro[]
Its contents are translated into `logback.xml` during the upgrade.
endif::pro[]

.What changes
[cols="1,1", options="header"]
|===
|Before (3.17 and earlier)|After (3.18 and later)

|`conf/log4j2.xml`|`conf/logback.xml`
|`conf/log4j2-debug.xml`|`conf/logback-debug.xml`
|`conf/log4j2-blank.xml`|`conf/logback-blank.xml`
|===

===== What the upgrade does for you

ifdef::pro[]
The SymmetricDS Pro setup program migrates your logging configuration for you. During an upgrade it:

. Converts your existing `conf/log4j2.xml` into `conf/logback.xml`, translating your appenders,
loggers, and levels into Logback syntax.
. Renames the old files to `log4j2.xml.deprecated`, `log4j2-debug.xml.deprecated`, and
`log4j2-blank.xml.deprecated`. They are kept for reference but are no longer read.
. Updates the startup scripts and service configuration to point at `logback.xml`.

In most cases no further action is needed. Your customizations -- extra loggers, changed levels, and
the standard file and console appenders -- carry over automatically.

TIP: If a `conf/logback.xml` file already exists before the upgrade, the setup program leaves it
alone and only retires the old Log4j files. This protects a configuration you wrote yourself.

*Review the conversion warnings.* A few Log4j2 constructs have no direct Logback equivalent. When
the setup program encounters one it completes the conversion, leaves that piece out, and records a
warning in `logs/install.log`. Search that file for `WARNING` after upgrading. You will see a warning
when:

* An appender type other than `Console`, `File`, `RollingFile`, or `SymRollingFile` was used. It is
not converted, and must be added to `logback.xml` by hand.
* An appender used a filter, or a layout other than `PatternLayout`. These are dropped and must be
reconfigured.
* A logger used the `FATAL` level. Logback has no `FATAL`, so it is converted to `ERROR`, which logs
more than before. Change it to `OFF` in `logback.xml` if you want the logger silenced completely.

Use the translation guide below to re-apply anything listed in those warnings.
endif::pro[]

ifndef::pro[]
Nothing is migrated automatically. After upgrading you need to:

. Re-apply any customizations from your old `log4j2.xml` to the new `conf/logback.xml`, using the
translation guide below.
. Delete or rename `conf/log4j2.xml`, `conf/log4j2-debug.xml`, and `conf/log4j2-blank.xml` so they are
not mistaken for live configuration files.
. Confirm that your startup scripts pass `-Dlogback.configurationFile` rather than
`-Dlog4j2.configurationFile`.
endif::pro[]

===== Behavior changes to be aware of

*The root level in the shipped configuration (default) changed from `INFO` to `ERROR`.* SymmetricDS 3.17
shipped `<Root level="INFO">` together with a `<Logger name="org" level="WARN"/>` entry.
SymmetricDS 3.18 ships `<root level="ERROR">` and sets the SymmetricDS loggers to `INFO` explicitly.
SymmetricDS logging is unchanged either way, but third-party libraries are quieter under the new
default. If you depend on output from a third-party library, add an explicit logger for it.

This applies to the `logback.xml` that ships with SymmetricDS -- a new installation, or an upgrade
where the file was not converted. If your configuration was converted from `log4j2.xml`, your
original root level is preserved.

*Configuration is now reloaded automatically.* The `<configuration scan="true">` attribute in
`logback.xml` means edits to the file take effect within about a minute without restarting
SymmetricDS. This is useful for turning on debug logging to diagnose a live problem, and for turning
it back off again afterwards.

===== Translating your configuration

Use this section when you are migrating a configuration by hand, or when you need to re-apply
something the SymmetricDS PRO setup program reported it could not convert.

Logback uses lowercase element names, drops the `<Appenders>` and `<Loggers>` wrapper elements, and
expresses several settings as child elements rather than attributes.

.Element and attribute mapping
[cols="1,1", options="header"]
|===
|Log4j2|Logback

|`<Configuration>`|`<configuration>`
|`<Appenders>` wrapper|Not used -- `<appender>` goes directly under `<configuration>`
|`<Loggers>` wrapper|Not used -- `<logger>` goes directly under `<configuration>`
|`<Logger name="x" level="INFO"/>`|`<logger name="x" level="INFO"/>`
|`<Root level="INFO">`|`<root level="INFO">`
|`<AppenderRef ref="ROLLING"/>`|`<appender-ref ref="ROLLING"/>`
|`<PatternLayout pattern="..."/>`|`<encoder><pattern>...</pattern></encoder>`
|`<SymRollingFile name="ROLLING" fileName="..."/>`|`<appender name="ROLLING" class="org.jumpmind.util.SymRollingFileAppender">` with a `<file>` child
|`filePattern="..."` attribute|`<rollingPolicy><fileNamePattern>...</fileNamePattern></rollingPolicy>`
|`<SizeBasedTriggeringPolicy size="20 MB"/>`|`<triggeringPolicy class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.SizeBasedTriggeringPolicy"><maxFileSize>20MB</maxFileSize></triggeringPolicy>`
|`<DefaultRolloverStrategy max="3"/>`|`<rollingPolicy class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.FixedWindowRollingPolicy">` with `<minIndex>` and `<maxIndex>`
|`${env:SYM_HOME:-.}`|`${SYM_HOME:-.}`
|`monitorInterval="30"`|`scan="true" scanPeriod="30 seconds"` on `<configuration>`
|`status="warn"` on `<Configuration>`|`<statusListener class="ch.qos.logback.core.status.NopStatusListener"/>`
|===

Levels are the same in both frameworks with one exception: Logback has no `FATAL` level. Use `OFF` to
silence a logger completely, or `ERROR` to keep error messages.

Details in the pattern layout also differ:

* `%c{1}` in Log4j2 prints the last segment of the logger name. The Logback equivalent is `%c{0}`.
* `%m%ex%n` in Log4j2 prints the message followed by the exception. Logback appends the exception
automatically, so `%m%n` is the equivalent.

===== Example

A Log4j2 configuration that writes to a rolling file, quiets Spring, and turns on debug logging for
SQL statements:

[source, xml]
----
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Configuration name="SYM" status="warn">
    <Appenders>
        <SymRollingFile name="ROLLING" fileName="${env:SYM_HOME:-.}/logs/symmetric.log"
                        filePattern="${env:SYM_HOME:-.}/logs/symmetric.log.%i" createOnDemand="true">
            <PatternLayout pattern="%d %p [%X{engineName}] [%c{1}] [%t] %m%ex%n" />
            <Policies>
                <SizeBasedTriggeringPolicy size="20 MB" />
            </Policies>
            <DefaultRolloverStrategy max="3" fileIndex="min" />
        </SymRollingFile>
    </Appenders>
    <Loggers>
        <Logger name="org.jumpmind" level="INFO" />
        <Logger name="org.springframework" level="ERROR" />
        <Logger name="org.atmosphere" level="FATAL" />
        <Logger name="org.jumpmind.db.sql" level="DEBUG" />
        <Root level="INFO">
            <AppenderRef ref="ROLLING" />
        </Root>
    </Loggers>
</Configuration>
----

The same configuration in Logback:

[source, xml]
----
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<configuration scan="true">
    <statusListener class="ch.qos.logback.core.status.NopStatusListener" />
    <appender name="ROLLING" class="org.jumpmind.util.SymRollingFileAppender">
        <file>${SYM_HOME:-.}/logs/symmetric.log</file>
        <rollingPolicy class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.FixedWindowRollingPolicy">
            <fileNamePattern>${SYM_HOME:-.}/logs/symmetric.log.%i</fileNamePattern>
            <minIndex>1</minIndex>
            <maxIndex>3</maxIndex>
        </rollingPolicy>
        <triggeringPolicy class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.SizeBasedTriggeringPolicy">
            <maxFileSize>20MB</maxFileSize>
        </triggeringPolicy>
        <encoder>
            <pattern>%d %p [%X{engineName}] [%c{0}] [%t] %m%n</pattern>
        </encoder>
    </appender>

    <logger name="org.jumpmind" level="INFO" />
    <logger name="org.springframework" level="ERROR" />
    <logger name="org.atmosphere" level="OFF" />
    <logger name="org.jumpmind.db.sql" level="DEBUG" />

    <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="ROLLING" />
    </root>
</configuration>
----

The `logback.xml` file that ships with SymmetricDS contains commented-out loggers for common
troubleshooting scenarios such as SQL statements, conflict resolution, and DDL comparison. Uncomment
the one you need rather than writing it from scratch.

===== If logging stops working after upgrade

* Confirm that `conf/logback.xml` exists and that `conf/log4j2.xml` has been renamed or removed.
* Confirm that `bin/setenv` (or `bin\setenv.bat` on Windows) sets `-Dlogback.configurationFile` and
that `conf/sym_service.conf` no longer references `log4j2.configurationFile`.
* If a specific library went quiet, it is most likely the root level change described above. Add an
explicit `<logger>` entry for it.
